# Orca's game patches for Super Smash Bros. Brawl (NTSC-U, revision 2), forced on in every session
# (Source/Core/Core/Orca/UX/GamePatches.h): `<address> <original|*> <value>`, hex. A line with an
# original is written only while that word is there. This file is hashed into the lobby key.
#
# Sections: the boot straight to the main menu; the main menu's Play Online, the game's own ONLINE
# page with no connection (ORCA.md "Online menu": with Orca's texture pack and its rewrite of the
# menu's text, UX/MenuText.h, nothing on it says Nintendo); everything unlocked; items off by
# default.
#
# Found with Orca's harness (ORCA_UX_PROBE watches, RAM dumps and Dolphin's disassembler); the
# unlock words are from the community "Global Brawl Netplay Settings" Gecko code shipped in
# Dolphin's GameSettings/RSBE01.ini (vBrawl's "Global Brawl Netplay Settings V1"), unlock part only.

# --- Boot straight to the main menu -------------------------------------------------------------

# scStrap (sora_scene, its process at 0x806CAC24, state at +0x28): skip the Wii Remote strap pages
# (state 3 -> 7, not 4) and the ESRB "online interactions" notice (state 11 -> 15, not 12). The
# scene still copies its packs to the NAND's /tmp and still waits for that copy (state 17) before
# it leaves, so everything the game later reads from /tmp is there.
806CAD50 41820010 48000010    # beq- +0x10 -> b +0x10 (state 3: always state 7)
806CAF80 41820010 48000010    # beq- +0x10 -> b +0x10 (state 11: always state 15)

# muBootNandTask (sora_menu_boot, loaded at 0x811A6000 on the boot scene; process 0x811A6300,
# state at +0x78): with no save, state 5 would open "Create a new save file?" (state 6 waits for
# it) and, on Yes, write a new save to the NAND. In its place: the game's own "continue without
# saving" (state 24 on No then Yes): clear the save flags (+0x48 &= 0xE0) and go to state 55, the
# end of the boot check. No dialog, no save written; a session's NAND is thrown away anyway. A
# session that seeds a save never reaches state 5. The six words are one group (GamePatches.h),
# written only while all six originals are there, the relocated `bl` included: other menu modules
# use this memory after the boot.
811A6430 80030068 88030048    # lbz   r0, 0x48(r3)
811A6434 83E3005C 54000634    # rlwinm r0, r0, 0, 24, 26
811A6438 2C000000 98030048    # stb   r0, 0x48(r3)
811A643C 4182001C 38000037    # li    r0, 55
811A6440 7C030378 90030078    # stw   r0, 0x78(r3)
811A6444 4AF193ED 48001204    # b     0x811A7648 (the process's end)

# scTitle (sora_scene, process 0x806CA098, state at +0x370, result at +0x36C, 0 = Start pressed):
# the title screen. Once state 2 has built it, it goes to the opening movie (3) or the logo (12);
# both become state 16, the title's own clean-up and exit, with result 0, which sqTitle takes to
# the main menu the usual way (through the scene change that frees the boot's menu modules: going to
# sqMenuMain straight from sqBoot runs out of the OverlayMenu heap).
806CA1F8 38000003 38000010    # li r0, 3 -> 16 (the opening movie)
806CA204 3800000C 38000010    # li r0, 12 -> 16 (the "press Start" logo)

# --- Main menu: Play Online, the game's own ONLINE page with no connection -----------------------

# The main menu's Wi-Fi button reads PLAY ONLINE (Orca's texture pack, Data/Sys/Orca/Textures, our
# own art) and opens the game's own ONLINE page (With Friends / With Anyone), relabelled and
# rewritten so nothing on it is Nintendo's (the breadcrumb by texture; the button's description
# by Orca's UX, UX/MenuText.h). Every way on from it leaves through the game's own exit to the local
# Versus character select; Orca reads which (UX/OnlineMenu.h) and tells the app. Nintendo's servers
# are never asked for: the connection window (muWifiCnctWndTask), which creates the network
# interface task, checks the save's Wi-Fi data and then connects, is never created; the Spectator
# and Options buttons (Options runs WiiConnect24 jobs) never show on the ONLINE page (the game
# hides them without a connection: nteGlobal's button flags) and nothing here shows them.
#
# Found under the harness (ORCA_UX_PROBE watches, RAM dumps and Dolphin's disassembler) with
# sora_menu_main.rel loaded at 0x81163780 (text at 0x8116384C); Project+'s own copy of the module
# sits 0x1480 higher (PPLUS32.patches).
#
# The cursor table of muProcTop (the top page's six buttons: 0 Group, 1 Solo, 2 Wi-Fi, 3 Vault,
# 4 Options, 5 Data) is the game's own again: Group moves down onto the button.
#
# muProcTop's update (0x811787F8): A on button 2 would create the connection window (mode 0) and
# wait for its result; instead the press takes the path every other button takes into its page
# (the cursor's proc, 3: muProcWifi). Two copies of the press check, one group each.
81178A58 A01D0042 A01D0042    # lhz    r0, 0x42(r29) (the cursor; guard)
81178A5C 28000002 28000002    # cmplwi r0, 2 (guard)
81178A60 4082007C 4800007C    # bne +0x7C -> b +0x7C: button 2 enters its page like the others

81178C30 A01D0042 A01D0042    # lhz    r0, 0x42(r29) (guard)
81178C34 28000002 28000002    # cmplwi r0, 2 (guard)
81178C38 4082007C 4800007C    # bne +0x7C -> b +0x7C

# muProcWifi (the ONLINE page): B would open the same window in mode 1, "Disconnect from Nintendo
# WFC?", and go back once it closed. Instead what the game does when that window returns: the back
# animation (0x806A5A58), next proc 0 (the top page), sub-state 3, return 1. Two copies (two input
# paths), one nine-word group each.
81179F48 4AEB30D1 7FA3EB78    # mr   r3, r29
81179F4C 3CA0811A 38800000    # li   r4, 0
81179F50 3CC0811A 4B52BB09    # bl   0x806A5A58 (the back animation)
81179F54 80630004 38000000    # li   r0, 0
81179F58 38A53C60 901D0634    # stw  r0, 0x634(r29) (next proc: the top page)
81179F5C 38C63C90 38000003    # li   r0, 3
81179F60 38800004 901D0664    # stw  r0, 0x664(r29) (sub-state)
81179F64 38E00000 38600001    # li   r3, 1
81179F68 4B276FDD 4800026C    # b    0x8117A1D4 (the function's end)

8117A148 4AEB2ED1 7FA3EB78    # mr   r3, r29
8117A14C 3CA0811A 38800000    # li   r4, 0
8117A150 3CC0811A 4B52B909    # bl   0x806A5A58
8117A154 80630004 38000000    # li   r0, 0
8117A158 38A53C60 901D0634    # stw  r0, 0x634(r29)
8117A15C 38C63C90 38000003    # li   r0, 3
8117A160 38800004 901D0664    # stw  r0, 0x664(r29)
8117A164 38E00000 38600001    # li   r3, 1
8117A168 4B276DDD 4800006C    # b    0x8117A1D4

# The ONLINE page's third button (Options, proc 28: muProcOptWifi, which runs WiiConnect24 jobs)
# and With Anyone's Spectator (exit code 29) show only when nteGlobal says the service allows them
# (isEnableButton 0x8014FEDC, flags 0x20/0x40 and 0x10). Without a connection it never does; here
# the answer is always no, so neither button is drawn or can take the cursor whatever that data
# holds. muProcWifi's init (0x811799B8):
81179A04 38600020 38600020    # li r3, 0x20 (guard)
81179A08 4AFD64D5 38600000    # bl isEnableButton -> li r3, 0
81179A0C 28030001 28030001    # (guard)
81179A10 4082000C 4082000C    # (guard)
81179A14 38000001 38000001    # (guard)
81179A18 48000018 48000018    # (guard)
81179A1C 38600040 38600040    # li r3, 0x40 (guard)
81179A20 4AFD64BD 38600000    # bl isEnableButton -> li r3, 0
# muProcWifiAnybody's init (0x81191D44):
81191D7C 38600010 38600010    # li r3, 0x10 (guard)
81191D80 4AFBE15D 38600000    # bl isEnableButton -> li r3, 0
81191D84 7C7F1B78 7C7F1B78    # mr r31, r3 (guard)

# sqMenuMain::setNext's table (sora_scene, resident; one entry per main-menu exit code, which the
# menu leaves at [0x805A0060]+0x284): every online sequence becomes sqVsMelee's entry (0x806DC618),
# the local Versus character select that Group > Brawl (code 1) opens. 24-27 are With Friends'
# modes (sqNetFriendList), 29 Spectator (sqNetAnyWatch), 30 With Anyone's Basic Brawl
# (sqNetAnyOkiraku), 31 its Team Battle (sqNetAnyTeamMelee): none of them is ever entered, so no
# online scene, no network thread, no DWC. The code itself stays as the menu wrote it: Orca reads it
# (30 Casual, 31 Ranked, 24-27 Friends). 28 is a guard. Project+ keeps Brawl's sora_scene, so its
# file has the same group.
80701A04 806DC8C8 806DC618    # code 24 (sqNetFriendList)
80701A08 806DC8C8 806DC618    # code 25
80701A0C 806DC8C8 806DC618    # code 26
80701A10 806DC8C8 806DC618    # code 27
80701A14 806DC940 806DC940    # code 28 (guard)
80701A18 806DC8E0 806DC618    # code 29 (sqNetAnyWatch)
80701A1C 806DC8F8 806DC618    # code 30 (sqNetAnyOkiraku)
80701A20 806DC910 806DC618    # code 31 (sqNetAnyTeamMelee)

# --- Everything unlocked --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The prize-unlock check (0x8004F978) reads any bit outside its 102-prize table as set for bits
# 0-11 of each word (the community code's choice).
8004FAE4 7C03002E 38000FFF    # lwzx r0, r3, r0 -> li r0, 0xFFF (the prize-unlock check)
8004E7D0 540307FE 38600001    # record +0x268 bit 0x01 -> always 1 (Solo: All-Star)
8004E7F4 5403FFFE 38600001    # record +0x268 bit 0x02 -> always 1 (Stadium: Boss Battles)
8004E818 5403F7FE 38600001    # record +0x268 bit 0x04 -> always 1 (community code)
8004E83C 5403EFFE 38600001    # record +0x268 bit 0x08 -> always 1 (community code)

# sqPrizeCheck (sora_scene, 0x806F5B64, state at +0x8): at boot it walks the prizes that are
# unlocked and not yet announced (the list at 0x806F5FB8: four tables, the last ones derived, such
# as co-op events from the roster) and, if any, shows them on scPrizeInfo before the menu. The
# list is still built (it marks what it finds as announced, as the game does), but state 10 always
# goes on to state 14, the menu, never to the announcement. The first table is skipped outright so
# its entries can never fill the 16-entry list before the derived tables have run.
806F603C 40820084 48000084    # bne- +0x84 -> b +0x84 (first table: skip the prize)
806F5EB0 40820010 48000010    # bne- +0x10 -> b +0x10 (state 10: never scPrizeInfo)

# gmGlobalRecord (0x9017B640) data, written every frame. The fighters: 3 bits each from bit 0 of
# the record (unlocked, shown on the character select, seen there: no "NEW!"), for the 14 secret
# fighters n = 0-13 of the table at 0x80407A40 (0x800AF350; n = 14 is a starter); the community code
# sets bits 0-8 (Marth, Luigi, Ness), here all 42. Then 0x80 bytes of state 2 at +0x1528, as the
# community code writes them.
9017B640 * FFFFFFFF
9017B644 * 000003FF
9017CB68 * 02020202
9017CB6C * 02020202
9017CB70 * 02020202
9017CB74 * 02020202
9017CB78 * 02020202
9017CB7C * 02020202
9017CB80 * 02020202
9017CB84 * 02020202
9017CB88 * 02020202
9017CB8C * 02020202
9017CB90 * 02020202
9017CB94 * 02020202
9017CB98 * 02020202
9017CB9C * 02020202
9017CBA0 * 02020202
9017CBA4 * 02020202
9017CBA8 * 02020202
9017CBAC * 02020202
9017CBB0 * 02020202
9017CBB4 * 02020202
9017CBB8 * 02020202
9017CBBC * 02020202
9017CBC0 * 02020202
9017CBC4 * 02020202
9017CBC8 * 02020202
9017CBCC * 02020202
9017CBD0 * 02020202
9017CBD4 * 02020202
9017CBD8 * 02020202
9017CBDC * 02020202
9017CBE0 * 02020202
9017CBE4 * 02020202
# The record's unlock bits (+0x18, the word array the check reads; +0x1C on is InfoAppFlagData, 2
# bits per prize, 3 = unlocked and announced): all set, so nothing is announced at boot and every
# stage is there.
9017B658 * FFFFFFFF
9017B65C * FFFFFFFF
9017B660 * FFFFFFFF
9017B664 * FFFFFFFF
9017B668 * FFFFFFFF
9017B66C * FFFFFFFF
9017B670 * FFFFFFFF
9017B674 * FFFFFFFF
# The prize states the check reads first (+0x24C, 102 prizes at 2 bits, bit 0 of each word first):
# all unlocked and announced, and nothing past the 204th bit.
9017B88C * FFFFFFFF
9017B890 * FFFFFFFF
9017B894 * FFFFFFFF
9017B898 * FFFFFFFF
9017B89C * FFFFFFFF
9017B8A0 * FFFFFFFF
9017B8A4 * 00000FFF

# --- Rules: items off by default ------------------------------------------------------------------

# gmGlobalRecord (0x9017B640) keeps the rules menu's item settings at +0x810, the record's menu data
# (GameGlobal::getGlobalRecordMenuDatap, 0x8004E5CC): +0 the item frequency (one byte: 0 None, 1
# Low, 2 Medium, 3 High) and +8 the item switch (64 bits, one per item). GameGlobal::init
# (0x8004C63C, its stores at 0x8004C9CC-0x8004C9D8) sets Medium with every item on before the
# first frame, so before any patch here can apply, and a session has no save to replace them: every
# match had items. Rules > Item Switch (muProcItemSwitch, in the resident rules module at
# 0x806A3A60: it reads these words at 0x806AA62C and writes them back at 0x806AA9B8) edits them in
# place, and a match takes its items from there. Instead, once: the frequency None, so no item
# appears. The switch is left as the game set it, so choosing a frequency there brings the usual
# items back. The group is written only while all four words hold the game's defaults; its second
# word (menu data +4, padding after the frequency byte: no load or store of record +0x811-0x817 in
# the DOL or the boot, menu and match modules) becomes 'ORCA', so it never lands again and a player
# who picks Medium with every item on keeps it (without the marker that choice went back to None
# in the same frame). All of it is emulated memory: a friend who drops in from the host's keyframe
# gets the host's settings and marker, and a re-run sees what the first run saw. Found with
# ORCA_UX_PROBE watches on the record and on the Item Switch page (its frequency at +0x678) under
# scripted visits; the community "Global Brawl Netplay Settings" code in Dolphin's
# GameSettings/RSBE01.ini writes the same words (and the stage list and the stock and time rules,
# not changed here).
9017BE50 02000000 00000000    # item frequency: Medium -> None
9017BE54 00000000 4F524341    # menu data +4 (unused): 'ORCA', the group has landed
9017BE58 FFFFFFFF FFFFFFFF    # item switch: every item on (guard only, left as is)
9017BE5C FFFFFFFF FFFFFFFF    # (guard only)
